# Line endings.
#
# The tree is LF everywhere -- in the repo and in the working copy. Windows-side
# editors kept rewriting files as CRLF, which surfaced as whole-file diffs on an
# otherwise untouched checkout. `text=auto` strips CR on the way into a commit
# and `eol=lf` writes LF back out on checkout, so neither side can reintroduce
# the churn regardless of a given clone's core.autocrlf.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are the one exception to the LF rule. cmd.exe parses them line by
# line as it executes, and a stray LF-only file can break the moment a label or
# a multi-line block is involved -- so these check out CRLF on every platform.
# The tree has none today (the scripted installers were dropped for the KiCad
# add-on package, which is applied by KiCad and runs nothing); the rule stays so
# that adding one cannot quietly get it wrong.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets -- never rewrite their bytes.
*.png binary
*.exe binary

# The solver builds. Only the Windows one ends in a suffix the rule above
# matches: the Linux and macOS builds are named for their machine
# (`monopole-linux-aarch64`, `monopole-macos`) and end in nothing at all. They
# are untracked in this repo (see .gitignore) and committed in the public one,
# and both check out through this file -- so the whole directory is named here
# rather than left to `text=auto`'s content sniffing, which would almost
# certainly call an executable binary and needs to be right every time, not
# almost.
#
# The directory rather than a list of names, because the list is
# emkit/sim/builds.py's and a second copy of it here would be one that could
# fall behind: nothing else is ever filed under binaries/.
binaries/** binary
